This happens to relate to a problem I was working on last month from the viewpoint of study design - how to come up with an unbiased design-based estimate from random or systematic detector clusters across a region when some clusters are truncated by boundary or overlap with non-habitat. With line transects you know the length of transect surveyed so you can make a size-specific adjustment/weighting. With SECR in 2-D there is no well-defined plot 'size'. Using a-hat seems ad hoc but maybe ad hoc is good enough. I have rigged some code and it seems to 'work' in the sense of giving plausible answers, but needs testing. See also attached. Essentially this implements your suggestion. I don't think there's any problem with the second variance component - we're estimating overall D = n/A where A is sum of cluster specific a_i and can sum their variances (assuming independence). Any comments?

