This isn't something I know about. My guess is you can just ignore it if the study is long enough. Changing occasion length can be done without data loss or changing the underlying model, but probably you're thinking of throwing away repeat detections at a site within 'occasions'. Perhaps a good idea if you have serious doubts about independence (a common issue with camera data).

People have combined movement models (leading to serial correlation of detection location) with SECR but I haven't paid much attention. There's the Royle et al transience paper in Population Ecology doi:10.1007/s10144-015-0524-z, and more recent work by Richard Glennie at St Andrews. Those could help you quantify the effect, but do you really care?

