Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I expect the transient trap response model will produce positively biased density estimates when the true model is a regular SCR observation model with transient activity centers in the process model. In the transient activity center paper, they show that density estimates from the null SCR model are unbiased when activity centers are transient (at least assuming their model structure). Behavioral response to capture models in general increase the D estimate, so I would think you would not want to use this model for this situation.

I'm not sure exactly what the underlying reasons for different density estimates from these two models might be, but perhaps it is something like this. When activity centers are transient, individuals have the same expected overall detection probability across time, just coming from different sets of traps. If you use a transient behavioral response, the expected overall detection probability for first capture is lower than the model without it. Because exposure to first capture makes up the majority of individual by trap events, the expected overall capture probability is lower than the model without a transient trap response. Therefore, the density estimate will be larger. Sounds plausible.

I'm with Murry on this, I don't think you need to worry about modeling this effect.

Ben

