> I expect the transient trap response model will produce positively biased density estimates when the true model is a regular SCR observation model with transient activity centers in the process model. In the transient activity center paper, they show that density estimates from the null SCR model are unbiased when activity centers are transient (at least assuming their model structure). Behavioral response to capture models in general increase the D estimate, so I would think you would not want to use this model for this situation.
>
> I'm not sure exactly what the underlying reasons for different density estimates from these two models might be, but perhaps it is something like this. When activity centers are transient, individuals have the same expected overall detection probability across time, just coming from different sets of traps. If you use a transient behavioral response, the expected overall detection probability for first capture is lower than the model without it. Because exposure to first capture makes up the majority of individual by trap events, the expected overall capture probability is lower than the model without a transient trap response. Therefore, the density estimate will be larger. Sounds plausible.
>
> I'm with Murry on this, I don't think you need to worry about modeling this effect.
>
> Ben
>
> On Sun, Dec 16, 2018 at 7:36 PM Murray Efford <murray...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > This isn't something I know about. My guess is you can just ignore it if the study is long enough. Changing occasion length can be done without data loss or changing the underlying model, but probably you're thinking of throwing away repeat detections at a site within 'occasions'. Perhaps a good idea if you have serious doubts about independence (a common issue with camera data).
> >
> > People have combined movement models (leading to serial correlation of detection location) with SECR but I haven't paid much attention. There's the Royle et al transience paper in Population Ecology doi:10.1007/s10144-015-0524-z, and more recent work by Richard Glennie at St Andrews. Those could help you quantify the effect, but do you really care?
> >
> > On Monday, December 17, 2018 at 12:19:10 PM UTC+13, matt.wa...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > Hi Murray,
> > >
> > > A great model for g0 in my study is a site specific, transient learned response.
> > > I am fairly confident that isn't trap happiness, as they cannot obtain anything from the lure (it's a camera trapping study). Rather, I think this is due to the way they move throughout their home range - spending time in particular pockets of their home range, then moving onto another (opposed to regularly travelling through their entire home range).
> > >
> > > I have two questions for you:
> > >
> > > 1) Is there a way to determine how many occasions this temporary response lasts for on average?
> > > Or to get this info, would I have to manually trawl through the capthist and say something like:  x% of recaptures happened within x occasions following the first detection at a site.
> > >
> > > 2) Given this is likely due to ranging behaviour rather than trap happiness, do you think I should change the occasion length to this period of time?
> > > i.e. instead of a 24 hour occasion, change it to a 5 day occasion"?
